doc=Document(src); changes=[]

def find(prefix):
 h=[p for p in doc.paragraphs if p.text.startswith(prefix)]
 if len(h)!=1: raise RuntimeError(f'{prefix!r}: {len(h)} hits')
 return h[0]

def replace(prefix,text,section,reason,source):
 p=find(prefix);old=p.text;p.text=text;changes.append([section,'REPLACE',old,text,reason,source]);return p

def after(anchor,text,style=None,section='',reason='',source=''):
 sty=next((q.style for q in doc.paragraphs if isinstance(style,str) and q.style.name==style),style) if style else anchor.style; p=doc.add_paragraph(text,style=sty);anchor._p.addnext(p._p);changes.append([section,'INSERT','',text,reason,source]);return p

replace('Canine chronic inflammatory enteropathy (CIE) is a spontaneous intestinal disease that may illuminate',
'''Canine chronic inflammatory enteropathy (CIE) is a spontaneous intestinal disease that may illuminate selected mechanisms of human Crohn disease, but the extent and limits of that correspondence are unresolved. We compared canine duodenal and human terminal-ileal single-cell transcriptomes using donor-level pseudobulk inference, one-to-one orthology, predefined lineage programs and three cell-state methods. In seven dogs, gene-level evidence was sparse, yet coordinated epithelial metabolic loss and immune inflammatory activation remained stable after individual dogs were omitted. The 27-participant human reference reproduced selected epithelial and inflammatory programs, whereas cross-species gene-effect correlations were weak and T-cell Reactome effects were negatively correlated. Cell-state methods disagreed, and no primary CONCORD lineage test remained significant after correction. Corrected subtype analyses supported epithelial heterogeneity but not a significant human macrophage direction reversal. A selection-aware matched-program calibration found 18 of 23 paired units directionally concordant, but its conditional upper-tail frequency was 0.0722 and was not confirmatory. Coeliac-disease, anatomical-region and mitochondrial-filtering analyses separated shared inflammation from disease-, anatomy- and quality-sensitive signals. TNF emerged as a translational benchmark, JAK1 as a canine validation candidate, and STAT3 and NDUFS1 as mechanistic nodes. Canine CIE therefore provides selective, lineage-resolved rather than global translational fidelity to Crohn disease.''',
'Abstract','Integrate corrected subtype and matched-background results without overstating significance','Phase12 HE12/HM3; S4 frozen source')

replace('To determine whether these program effects were carried by one animal,',
'''To determine whether these program effects were carried by one animal, we repeated the complete donor-level analysis after omitting each dog in turn. Twenty of the 25 lineage-by-program combinations met the frozen robust criterion and five were moderately stable (Fig. 2c and Supplementary Table 4). This leave-one-dog-out result supports internal stability but is not independent biological replication. The 25 units were frozen after this canine analysis and before formal human testing; all later cross-species classifications are consequently conditional on a canine-outcome-informed program set.''',
'Results','Correct panel citation and preserve selection-boundary language','Phase28 panel audit; Phase9 selection audit')

replace('We next asked whether the canine programs could be evaluated in a larger human disease reference.',
'''We next asked whether the canine programs could be evaluated in a larger human disease reference. GSE266616 contained 237,878 terminal-ileum cells from 36 participants across all study conditions. The predefined primary comparison used 132,041 cells from 16 healthy participants and 11 participants with active Crohn disease; within the three target lineages it included 80,562 epithelial, 21,378 T and 3,772 myeloid cells. This distinction separates the full-atlas cell count from the donor set used for primary inference. The accession is reported directly because the publication linked in the GEO citation field does not describe this dataset.''',
'Results','Separate full-atlas and primary-contrast denominators and avoid misattributing the dataset publication','Phase28 count reconciliation; GSE266616 provenance audit')

anchor=find('The human effects were not wholly dependent')
ann=after(anchor,
'''Fine-label recovery was incomplete and condition-dependent (Supplementary Fig. S1). Within the frozen 21,378-cell broad-T universe, 15,670 cells had valid recovered author labels; 15,233 were T-related and 437 carried author NK/ILC identities. Recovery was 85.3% in healthy T cells and 56.5% in Crohn T cells. Corresponding author-label recovery was 67.5% versus 37.8% for epithelial cells and 70.9% versus 66.2% for myeloid cells. We therefore treated subtype results as exploratory sensitivity analyses rather than an unbiased census of cell states.''',
section='Results',reason='Explicitly integrate Supplementary Figure S1 and its denominator/selection limitation',source='S1d_human_author_label_recovery.csv; frozen S1 legend')
after(ann,
'''After enforcing the predefined requirement that every included participant contribute at least 20 cells, formal epithelial subtype models contained 11 healthy and 6 Crohn participants for Early Enterocyte, 13 and 6 for Enterocyte, 13 and 6 for Ileum Goblet, and 8 and 5 for transit-amplifying cells (Supplementary Figs. S2-S3). Eleven of 12 epithelial state-program tests passed the 12-test Benjamini-Hochberg family. Early Enterocyte, Enterocyte and transit-amplifying cells showed lower oxidative phosphorylation, mitochondrial respiration and xenobiotic metabolism in active Crohn disease. Ileum Goblet cells differed: mitochondrial respiration (NES = 2.30, BH q = 2.4 × 10−10) and oxidative phosphorylation (NES = 1.66, q = 0.0200) were higher, whereas xenobiotic metabolism was positive but not significant (NES = 1.20, q = 0.144). Both respiratory directions were preserved in all 19 valid Goblet leave-one-participant-out fits. The Macrophage model contained 13 healthy and 6 Crohn participants, but none of its three programs passed BH correction: inflammatory response was weakly positive (NES = 1.08, q = 0.675), whereas TNF/NF-κB and TLR4 were weakly negative (NES = −0.91 and −0.92; q = 0.675 for both). Recovered-cohort broad-myeloid estimates retained the positive primary directions. These analyses support epithelial state heterogeneity but neither conserved macrophage activation nor an opposite macrophage mechanism.''',
section='Results',reason='Integrate Phase12 eligibility-corrected subtype estimates and S2-S3 conclusions',source='HUMAN_EPITHELIAL_RESULTS_PHASE12.csv; HM3; Phase12 LODO')

anchor=find('The scale dependence was biologically structured.')
after(anchor,
'''Because the 25 programs were selected from canine results before human testing, we calibrated the observed cross-species direction agreement against selection-aware matched backgrounds (Supplementary Fig. S4). Eighteen of 23 paired-testable units were concordant (78.3%) versus a mean of 63.78% across 5,000 saved K20 matched draws; 360 draws were at least as large, giving a plus-one conditional upper-tail frequency of 0.0722. Excluding all five match-limited units left 14 of 18 concordant, with a 65.33% background mean and an upper-tail frequency of 0.1706. This diagnostic calibration shows that the observed advantage was not wholly driven by the five match-limited units, but it did not provide confirmatory evidence of excess conservation; no complete participant-label permutation P value was obtained.''',
section='Results',reason='Integrate Supplementary Figure S4 with non-confirmatory interpretation',source='S4a/S4b/S4c frozen source data')

replace('CONCORD, which learns a shared representation without the same protein-language prior,',
'''CONCORD, which learns a shared representation without the same protein-language prior, gave the largest primary-seed correspondence in myeloid cells (cosine = 0.5873, empirical P = 0.0197), with weaker epithelial (cosine = 0.3672, P = 0.0857) and T-cell (cosine = 0.0849, P = 0.783) results.[29] Benjamini-Hochberg correction across the three primary lineage tests gave q = 0.0591, 0.1286 and 0.783 for Myeloid, Epithelial and T, respectively; no lineage reached q < 0.05. The methods therefore did not converge on one conserved lineage. The comparatively stronger myeloid CONCORD result was consistent with shared inflammatory programs, whereas the seed-sensitive SATURN T-cell result could not overturn metabolic divergence in ranked genes and pathways. State correspondence is consequently method-dependent and is complementary evidence rather than a consensus classifier.''',
'Results','Align CONCORD interpretation with corrected BH family and latest figure','Phase13/Phase28 CONCORD audit')

replace('Only after state and boundary analyses did we assign final interpretations',
'''Only after the state and boundary analyses did we assign final interpretations to all 25 lineage-by-program units. Ten were classified as generic mucosal inflammatory programs, seven as canine-divergent, four as dog-Crohn differentiating hints, three as region-associated or uncertain and one as method-dependent or uncertain (Supplementary Table 6). Three units had high, ten moderate and twelve limited confidence. The categories are mutually exclusive products of the frozen rules; “differentiating hint” denotes a bounded pattern in these datasets, not established disease specificity.''',
'Results','Remove nonexistent Figure 5d citation and preserve classification boundary','Phase28 panel reconciliation')

anchor=find('Epithelial correspondence was biologically plausible')
after(anchor,
'''Subtype resolution sharpened this epithelial-myeloid boundary. After exclusion of five participant-state pseudobulks with fewer than 20 cells, the disease-associated increase in Ileum Goblet respiratory programs remained directionally stable, while Early Enterocyte and transit-amplifying metabolic programs remained negative and BH-significant. Goblet xenobiotic metabolism was not supported after correction. Corrected human Macrophage effects were small and nonsignificant, while broad-myeloid inflammatory programs remained positive in the recovered cohort. Subtype estimates therefore qualify, rather than overwrite, the primary broad-lineage effects.''',
section='Discussion',reason='Reflect Phase12 eligibility correction and revised epithelial/macrophage boundary',source='Phase12 corrected results and eligibility audit')

anchor=find('Several limitations define what remains unknown.')
replace('Several limitations define what remains unknown.',
'''Several limitations define what remains unknown. The canine cohort comprised three healthy and four CIE dogs, and leave-one-dog-out stability is not independent replication. The 25 programs were selected after canine results and LODO stability, then frozen before formal human testing; classifications avoid human-side tuning but remain conditional on canine-side selection. The matched-background calibration was also non-confirmatory (conditional upper-tail frequency 0.0722; 0.1706 after excluding five match-limited units), and no complete participant-label permutation test was available. Broad lineages can conceal subtype and compositional effects, while author-label recovery was incomplete and condition-dependent. Dog duodenum was compared with human terminal ileum, and healthy-region sensitivity with three paired donors cannot resolve that mismatch. Human treatment sensitivities are observational, and untreated-Crohn subtype sensitivities were not estimable under the five-per-group rule. One-to-one orthology excludes paralogous and species-specific biology. State-method disagreement and no adjusted-significant CONCORD test caution against a single latent-space conclusion. Larger independent canine cohorts, matched regions and prospective program testing are required.''',
'Discussion','Integrate S1/S4, subtype eligibility and treatment-estimability limitations','Phase12 eligibility audit; S4 frozen audit')

replace('The primary Human reference was GSE266616.',
'''The primary human reference was GSE266616. The complete atlas contained 237,878 terminal-ileum cells from 36 participants across healthy, active-inflamed, adjacent and non-inflamed conditions. The formal primary contrast retained 132,041 cells from 16 healthy participants and 11 with active Crohn disease. Active-case metadata recorded three untreated, five anti-TNF-exposed and three anti-IL-12/23-exposed participants. Participant pseudobulk was used; five fixed treatment sensitivities were not treated as independent validation cohorts. GSE266616 is cited by accession because its GEO citation field points to a publication that does not describe this dataset.''',
'Methods','Correct dataset provenance and preserve denominators','GSE266616 provenance audit; primary metadata')

anchor=find('The primary human reference was GSE266616.')
h=after(anchor,'Human subtype annotation recovery and eligibility',style='Heading 3',section='Methods',reason='Add reproducible method for S1-S3 subtype analysis',source='Phase12 workflow')
after(h,
'''Recovered author cell identifiers were first mapped to the complete, unfiltered h5ad observation index and only then used to index the backed raw-UMI count layer. Counts were summed by participant and author state. Before any design matrix was constructed, a participant-state pseudobulk was retained only if it contained at least 20 cells; formal state models additionally required at least five healthy and five active-Crohn participants. The final models included 11/6 participants for Early Enterocyte, 13/6 for Enterocyte, 13/6 for Ileum Goblet, 8/5 for transit-amplifying cells and 13/6 for Macrophage. edgeR used filterByExpr, independent TMM normalization, robust dispersion estimation and quasi-likelihood testing with design ~ condition. Human-native predefined programs were tested with the frozen fgseaMultilevel ranking and size rules. BH correction was applied across the 12 epithelial tests and separately across the three macrophage tests. Leave-one-participant-out refits re-applied the cell and five-per-group gates after each omission; non-estimable fits were retained as such and were not replaced by cell-level inference.''',
section='Methods',reason='Document full-index mapping and actual pre-fit n_cells filter',source='phase12_refit_affected_human_subtypes.R; 01_ELIGIBILITY_AUDIT.csv')

anchor=find('Dog–Human orthologues were restricted')
h=after(anchor,'Selection-aware matched-program calibration',style='Heading 3',section='Methods',reason='Add reproducible S4 method',source='S4 frozen audit')
after(h,
'''To assess whether concordance of the dog-derived 25-program set exceeded a matched background under the same selection structure, we retained the 23 units with paired testable dog and human directions and drew exactly 5,000 K20 matched program sets from the frozen matching neighbourhoods. The observed concordance was compared with the saved draw distribution using a plus-one conditional upper-tail frequency, (draws at least as concordant + 1)/(5,000 + 1). K10 and K30 neighbourhoods and exclusion of five match-limited units were prespecified diagnostics. These calculations condition on the selected program identities and matching scheme; they are not a participant-label permutation test and were not interpreted as confirmatory P values.''',
section='Methods',reason='State exact S4 sampling and inferential boundary',source='S4 saved draws and matching audit')

replace('singIST v1.0.2 used 46 Human reference models',
'''singIST v1.0.2 was applied to 46 human reference models using one validation rule.[67] SATURN used ESM1b layer 33 protein embeddings, 8,000 condition-blind highly variable genes, 2,000 macrogenes, a 256-dimensional latent space, 200 pretraining and 50 metric-learning epochs, batch size 4,096 and 28,046 balanced cells (14,023 per species), across three random seeds.[68] CONCORD v1.0.8 used 10,000 highly variable genes, the same balanced cells, a 300-dimensional latent space, species as domain, encoder width 1,000, batch size 256, 15 epochs, mask rates 0.4/0.2, temperature 0.3, learning rate 0.01 with decay 0.97, and decoder/classifier disabled, across three seeds.[29] Disease-vector cosines and method-specific empirical nulls were evaluated separately for each lineage. Seeds assessed computational stability, not biological replication.''',
'Methods','Replace obsolete CONCORD implementation details with executed parameters','Phase13 scripts and Phase11 audited Methods')

replace('All disease-effect inference used donor- or participant-level pseudobulk.',
'''All disease-effect inference used donor- or participant-level pseudobulk. Gene-wise FDR used Benjamini-Hochberg correction within each differential analysis; primary program FDR was adjusted within gene-set collection, whereas human epithelial and macrophage subtype programs used the prespecified HE12 and HM3 families.[69] Spearman correlations were descriptive unless accompanied by a prespecified permutation procedure. CONCORD reported raw empirical P values and BH q values across three primary lineage tests. The S4 matched-background upper-tail quantities were conditional calibration frequencies, not participant-label permutation P values. Sensitivity analyses were not pooled as biological replicates. No post hoc threshold or model was selected to improve an outcome.''',
'Methods','Synchronize multiple-testing families and S4 statistical interpretation','Phase12 results; S4 audit')

# Replace all main-figure legends with the latest audited wording.
legend_replacements={
'Canine CIE and Human Crohn disease were analysed as parallel comparisons.':'''Canine CIE and human Crohn disease were analysed as parallel disease comparisons. The canine dataset comprised duodenal single-cell transcriptomes from three healthy and four CIE dogs (38,186 cells). The complete human terminal-ileum atlas contained 36 participants and 237,878 cells; the predefined primary contrast used 16 healthy participants and 11 participants with active Crohn disease (132,041 cells). Donors or participants—not cells—were biological replicates, and raw counts were aggregated by individual and lineage. The species were compared in Epithelial, T and Myeloid lineages at three resolutions: strict one-to-one ortholog effects, predefined disease-program effects and cell-state representations. Leave-one-dog-out and mitochondrial-QC analyses assessed canine robustness; healthy-region, coeliac-disease and external canine analyses defined interpretation boundaries. No global similarity score or inferential test was applied to this schematic.''',
'a, Canine CIE program effects for 25 predefined':'''a, Canine CIE program effects (CIE minus healthy) for 25 predefined lineage-by-program units from donor-level pseudobulk data for seven dogs (three healthy and four CIE). b, Human program effects (active Crohn minus healthy) for the same program identities from the 27-participant primary contrast (16 healthy and 11 active Crohn; 132,041 cells before lineage restriction). Points are coloured and shaped by lineage; “n.t.” denotes a program that did not meet the gene-set size rule. Differential expression used robust edgeR quasi-likelihood models, and enrichment used fgseaMultilevel on sign(logFC) × sqrt(F) ranks, with Benjamini-Hochberg correction within species, lineage and collection (Methods). c, Independent support summaries. Canine leave-one-dog-out analysis classified 20 of 25 programs as robust and five as moderately stable. Human Level1 cell counts (Epithelial 144,941; T 41,200; Myeloid 6,570) describe the complete 36-participant atlas, whereas tested-gene and FDR counts derive from the primary 27-participant contrast: Epithelial 5,465/18,142 (30.1%), T 359/10,939 (3.3%) and Myeloid 708/6,164 (11.5%). These evidence types and denominators were interpreted separately; cells were not biological replicates.''',
'a, Canine CIE versus human Crohn disease-program effects':'''a, Canine CIE versus human Crohn disease-program effects for 23 of 25 predefined lineage-by-program units with paired testable normalized enrichment scores (NES), derived from seven dogs and 27 human participants. Points are coloured and shaped by lineage; zero lines separate same- and opposite-direction quadrants, and the diagonal marks equal NES. Eight labels highlight biologically informative programs. Epithelial and T oxidative-phosphorylation units were not testable and were not assigned zero. b, Spearman correlations between canine and human gene-level logFC values in lineage-specific strict one-to-one-ortholog universes: Epithelial ρ = 0.1474, T ρ = 0.0159 and Myeloid ρ = 0.1011. c, Collection-specific correlations between paired pathway NES values. Hallmark, Reactome v97 and GO-BP correlations were Epithelial ρ = 0.5582, 0.3340 and 0.2043; T ρ = 0.2342, −0.2619 and 0.2136; and Myeloid ρ = 0.4761, 0.0137 and 0.2979. No arithmetic mean across collections is shown. Panels b and c are descriptive effect correlations.''',
'Disease vectors were calculated from donor centroids':'''Disease vectors were calculated from donor centroids, giving equal weight to seven dogs and 27 human participants. a, Canine-human disease-vector cosine similarity for Epithelial, T and Myeloid states estimated independently by SATURN and CONCORD across three random seeds. Small symbols show seeds, horizontal segments the seed range and large black-edged symbols the median; circles denote SATURN and diamonds CONCORD. b, Primary-seed CONCORD cosine relative to the joint donor-label null distribution. Grey segments show the 2.5th-97.5th null percentiles, ticks the null median and coloured symbols the observed cosine. Two-sided empirical P values were Epithelial 0.0857, T 0.783 and Myeloid 0.0197; Benjamini-Hochberg q values across these three primary lineage tests were 0.1286, 0.783 and 0.0591, respectively. No lineage reached q < 0.05. c, singIST human reference-model quality: all 46 models were weak and none met the FDR < 0.10 criterion. The panels use different representations and were interpreted separately.''',
'a, Canine epithelial program NES under mitochondrial-content':'''a, Canine epithelial program NES under mitochondrial-content thresholds MT80, MT60 and MT40 (<80%, <60% and <40% mitochondrial reads). All thresholds retained seven dogs and required at least 20 epithelial cells per donor. Solid trajectories connect comparable results; the dashed TNF/NF-κB trajectory marks its direction reversal at MT40. b, Healthy human anatomical-region effects from three donors with paired duodenum and terminal ileum samples, fitted as ~donor + region. Positive NES indicates ileum-higher. Filled circles indicate alignment with the dog-human disease direction, open circles opposition and crosses discordant donor directions. c, Human Crohn NES (27 participants) versus active coeliac-disease NES from 32 subjects (11 controls and 21 active cases) for 23 paired testable programs. Colour and shape encode lineage; filled symbols denote dog-human same-direction programs and open symbols opposite-direction programs. Same-sign quadrants indicate shared Crohn/coeliac direction, but absence of sharing does not prove Crohn specificity. Technical-QC, healthy-region and disease-context results were interpreted separately.''',
'Four prioritized nodes are shown across Human therapeutic':'''Four prioritized nodes are shown across human therapeutic, cross-species program, target-level and canine evidence. Filled, half-filled and open grey circles indicate strong, partial and absent or not-assessable evidence; diamonds indicate a canine pilot intervention. “Pilot”, “Class-level” and “pSTAT3” mark canine evidence type, and lineage symbols indicate primary support. Roles are rule based, not numerical scores or an efficacy ranking: TNF, positive translational benchmark; JAK1, candidate for canine CIE validation; STAT3, immune mechanistic node; and NDUFS1, epithelial metabolic node. The TNF canine evidence derives from a small randomized, blinded, placebo-controlled pilot study under a historical canine IBD definition. It neither establishes anti-TNF as standard contemporary CIE therapy nor supports direct transfer of a human biologic, dose, safety profile or cross-reactivity to dogs. No new statistical test was performed.'''
}
